As for the selection process, each candidate is chosen separately through
 tournament selection, where the fittest of (3-7) random candidates wins.
 This allows trees to survive in a population that have not yet been optimised
 but may carry important genes in the future.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:gp-Evolutionary-concepts"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Dennoch ist es sehr entscheidend, wie sich eine Population zusammensetzt,
 abgewogen werden muss vor allem zwischen Reproduktion von Genen durch Selektion
, die wichtig ist um einen funktionierenden Genpool aufrecht zu erhalten,
 und Mutation, die für eine genetische Vielfalt sorgt.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
We use the tournament selection, which randomly selects 3-7 random candidates
 and returns the fittest.
 This allows trees to survive in a population that have not yet been optimised
 but may carry important genes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A new population is largely generated from a selection of the best candidates
 of the last generation.
 This allows successful gene sequences to multiply.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
New candidates are generated by random gene mutations without having to
 search for a completely new solution, and by crossing candidates,.
 The rest of the population is replenished with randomly generated candidates
 to guarantee a constant stream of new solutions.
\end_layout

